{"records":[{"id":"rhsw3nbjhz","title":"Essential gene profiles for human pluripotent stem cells identify uncharacterized genes and substrate dependencies. Mair et al.","description":"Human pluripotent stem cells (hPSCs) provide an invaluable tool for modeling diseases and hold promise for regenerative medicine. For understanding pluripotency and lineage differentiation mechanisms, a critical first step involves systematically cataloging genes that are indispensable for hPSC maintenance and proliferation. To map genetic determinants of hPSC fitness, we performed genome-scale loss-of-function screens in an inducible Cas9 H1 hPSC line cultured on feeder cells and feeder-free to identify essential genes. Among these, we found FOXH1 and VENTX, genes that encode transcription factors previously implicated in stem cell biology, as well as an uncharacterized gene, C22orf43/DRICH1. hPSCs essential genes are substantially different from other cell lines, and gene essentiality in hPSCs is highly context-dependent with respect to different growth substrates. Our CRISPR screens establish parameters for genome-wide screens in hPSCs, which will facilitate the characterization of unappreciated regulators of hPSC biology to understand the genetic wiring of hPSC fitness and pluripotency.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-01-24T11:57:50.932Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rhsw3nbjhz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rhsw3nbjhz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Barbara","last_name":"Mair","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jelena","last_name":"Tomic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sanna","last_name":"Masud","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jason","last_name":"Moffat","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Stem Cell","Functional Genomics","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"8xhv88c7wt","title":"Genome-wide screening in human kidney organoids identifies developmental and disease-related aspects of nephrogenesis","description":"Human organoids allow the study of proliferation, lineage specification, and three-dimensional tissue development. Here, we present a genome-wide CRISPR screen in iPSC-derived kidney organoids. The combination of inducible genome editing, longitudinal sampling and endpoint sorting of tubular and stromal cells generated a complex, high-quality dataset uncovering a broad spectrum of insightful biology from early development to ‘adult’ epithelial morphogenesis. Our functional dataset allows improving mesoderm induction by ROCK inhibition, contains monogenetic and complex trait kidney disease genes, confirms two additional CAKUT genes (CCDC170 and MYH7B), and provides a large candidate list of ciliopathy-related genes. Finally, the identification of a cis-inhibitory effect of Jagged1 controlling epithelial proliferation shows how mosaic knockouts in pooled CRISPR screening can discover ways of communication between heterogeneous cell populations in complex tissues. Collectively, these data serve both as a rich resource for the kidney community and as a benchmark for future iPSC-derived organoid CRISPR screens.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-11-29T13:48:59.335Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8xhv88c7wt","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8xhv88c7wt"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Rosemarie","last_name":"Ungricht","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Philipp","last_name":"Hoppe","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Screening","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"bpcpfns6vb","title":"Global genetic interaction network of a human cell maps conserved principles and informs functional interpretation of gene co-essentiality profiles. Billmann et al.","description":"Deciphering how genes interact within human cells is essential for understanding their functional wiring and the development of targeted therapeutic strategies. In this study, we present a genome-scale map of genetic interactions in the human haploid cell line HAP1, based on CRISPR-based perturbation of ~ 4 million gene pairs. The resulting network comprises ~89,000 high-confidence gene-gene interactions, organizing genes into hierarchical modules corresponding to protein complexes and pathways, biological processes, and cellular compartments, mirroring principles observed in yeast and highlighting the functional architecture of a human cell. This large-scale genetic network complements the DepMap gene co-essentiality network by capturing unique functional information, uncovering roles of previously uncharacterized genes, and identifying molecular determinants of cancer cell line-specific genetic dependencies. This study presents a general data-driven strategy analysis for systematically exploring the roles of genes and their functional connections in human cell lines.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-06-18T10:15:12.084Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/bpcpfns6vb","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/bpcpfns6vb"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Maximilian","last_name":"Billmann","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michael","last_name":"Costanzo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xiang","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Arshia Z.","last_name":"Hassan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mahfuzur","last_name":"Rahman","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kevin R.","last_name":"Brown","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Katherine S.","last_name":"Chan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Amy Hin Yan","last_name":"Tong","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Carles","last_name":"Pons","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Henry N.","last_name":"Ward","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Catherine","last_name":"Ross","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jolanda","last_name":"van Leeuwen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michael","last_name":"Aregger","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Keith A.","last_name":"Lawson","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Barbara","last_name":"Mair","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Amy F.","last_name":"Roth","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nesli E.","last_name":"Sen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Duncan T.","last_name":"Forster","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Guihong","last_name":"Tan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Patricia","last_name":"Mero","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sanna N.","last_name":"Masud","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yoonkyu","last_name":"Lee","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Magali","last_name":"Aguilera-Uribe","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Matej","last_name":"Usaj","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sylvia M. T.","last_name":"Almeida","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kamaldeep","last_name":"Aulakh","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Urvi","last_name":"Bhojoo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Saba","last_name":"Birkadze","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nathaniel","last_name":"Budijono","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xunhui","last_name":"Cai","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Joseph J.","last_name":"Caumanns","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jordan J.","last_name":"Chalmers","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Megha","last_name":"Chandrashekhar","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Daniel","last_name":"Chang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ryan","last_name":"Climie","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kuheli","last_name":"Dasgupta","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Adrian","last_name":"Drazic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jose I.","last_name":"Echenique","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rafael","last_name":"Gacesa","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Adrian","last_name":"Granda Farias","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Andrea","last_name":"Habsid","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ira","last_name":"Horecka","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kristin","last_name":"Kantautas","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Fenghu","last_name":"Ji","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Dae-Kyum","last_name":"Kim","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Seon Yong","last_name":"Lee","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wendy","last_name":"Liang","orc